
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Systemd-networkd</strong> is a network configuration manager in systemd.<br>It can manage the networks of a Linux system, and configure network devices.<br>Its configuration is based on INI-style configuration files.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Systemd-networkd can be managed at runtime using the <code>networkctl</code> tool.<br></p>



<span id="more-9526"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In this example you create a network bridge device.<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">Physical device: enp1s0</div><div class="cm-line">Bridge device:   bridge0</div><div class="cm-line">Interface IP:    10.10.0.10/24</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>You have to change this information for your own system.</strong><br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Create the following files in <code>/etc/systemd/network/</code>:<br></p>


<div style="--line-number-gutter-width: 1ch" class="show-line-numbers wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo touch /etc/systemd/network/10-bridge0.netdev</div><div class="cm-line">sudo touch /etc/systemd/network/20-enp1s0.network</div><div class="cm-line">sudo touch /etc/systemd/network/30-bridge0.network</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Configuration files are processed in lexicographical order.<br>For <code>.network</code> files, the first matching file is applied to an interface.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Create the <strong>bridge network device</strong> in <code>/etc/systemd/network/10-bridge0.netdev</code> with<br>the following configuration:<br></p>


<div style="--line-number-gutter-width: 1ch" class="show-line-numbers wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">[NetDev]</div><div class="cm-line">Name=bridge0</div><div class="cm-line">Kind=bridge</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Attach your physical network interface to the bridge by configuring <code>/etc/systemd/network/20-enp1s0.network</code>:<br></p>


<div style="--line-number-gutter-width: 1ch" class="show-line-numbers wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">[Match]</div><div class="cm-line">Name=enp1s0</div><div class="cm-line"></div><div class="cm-line">[Network]</div><div class="cm-line">Bridge=bridge0</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Configure the <code>bridge0</code> interface in <code>/etc/systemd/network/30-bridge0.network</code>:<br></p>


<div style="--line-number-gutter-width: 1ch" class="show-line-numbers wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">[Match]</div><div class="cm-line">Name=bridge0</div><div class="cm-line"></div><div class="cm-line">[Network]</div><div class="cm-line">Address=10.10.0.10/24</div><div class="cm-line">Gateway=10.10.0.1</div><div class="cm-line">DNS=8.8.8.8</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Check your existing network configuration.</strong><br>Depending on your installation, the <code>enp1s0</code> interface might already be configured<br>through ifupdown (<code>/etc/network/interfaces</code>) or NetworkManager.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Make sure the interface is not managed by another network management service.<br>Remove or disable the conflicting configuration before proceeding.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Warning:</strong><br></p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Switching network management services remotely is a potentially disruptive operation.<br>If the bridge configuration fails, you might lose SSH access.</p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Enable and start <code>systemd-networkd</code>:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo systemctl enable --now systemd-networkd</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Verify your configuration:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo networkctl list</div><div class="cm-line">IDX LINK    TYPE     OPERATIONAL SETUP     </div><div class="cm-line">  1 lo      loopback carrier     unmanaged</div><div class="cm-line">  2 enp1s0  ether    enslaved    configured</div><div class="cm-line">  3 bridge0 bridge   routable    configured</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Check your bridge&#8217;s status:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo networkctl status bridge0</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the system uses <code>systemd-resolved</code>, you should enable it too:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo systemctl enable --now systemd-resolved</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Where appropriate, configure <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> to point to the <code>systemd-resolved</code> stub resolver:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo ln -sf /run/systemd/resolve/stub-resolv.conf /etc/resolv.conf</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Don&#8217;t blindly execute the symlink command on an existing system.</strong><br>Check the current <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> arrangement first.<br></p>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><code>systemd-networkd</code> configures the interface and supplies DNS configuration.</li>



<li><code>systemd-resolved</code> handles DNS resolution.</li>
</ol>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Reload the configuration:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo networkctl reload</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you have to reconfigure a relevant interface:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo networkctl reconfigure bridge0</div><div class="cm-line">sudo networkctl reconfigure enp1s0</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can reboot the server to see if the config survives it:<br></p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">sudo reboot
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The machine should boot up with the expected interface configuration.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Alternatively, you can configure the bridge to obtain its network configuration through DHCP:<br></p>


<div style="--line-number-gutter-width: 1ch" class="show-line-numbers wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">[Network]</div><div class="cm-line">DHCP=yes</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you need more help, use:<br></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><code>man systemd-networkd</code> for general help.</li>



<li><code>man systemd.network</code> for network configuration.</li>



<li><code>man systemd.netdev</code> for device settings.</li>



<li><code>man networkctl</code> for run time operations.</li>
</ul>



